#!/bin/sh

# quickshell-nothp
#
# Starts quickshell with Transparent Huge Pages (THP) disabled for that
# process only. Nothing system-wide changes, and other programs are untouched.
#
# Why: with THP set to "always" in the kernel, each thread's stack and
# allocator area can get a full 2 MB huge page even if it only needs a few KB.
# Quickshell has many threads, so the waste adds up. Disabling THP for this
# process avoids that and lowers its RAM usage.
#
# How it works:
#   1. python calls prctl(41, 1, 0, 0, 0)
#        - 41 = PR_SET_THP_DISABLE (the "no huge pages for me" switch)
#        - 1  = turn it on
#   2. python then calls os.execvp, which replaces itself with quickshell.
#      Python is gone afterward and only quickshell keeps running.
#   3. The "no THP" flag is kept across that replacement, so quickshell
#      starts with it already set.
#
# Usage: run this instead of quickshell. Any arguments are passed along,
# e.g.  quickshell-nothp -c myconfig
#
# Check that it worked (should print "THP_enabled: 0"):
#   grep THP_enabled /proc/$(pgrep -x quickshell)/status

# "exec" replaces this shell script with python, so no extra process stays
# around. The qs executable is used so the script can never
# accidentally call itself. "$@" forwards all arguments to quickshell.
exec python3 -c 'import ctypes,os,sys;ctypes.CDLL(None).prctl(41,1,0,0,0);os.execvp(sys.argv[1],sys.argv[1:])' qs "$@"
